0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,0,0,0,2,1,2,1,2,2,1,3,3,3,3,0,0,0,2,0]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"The grid is made of solid colour-3 rectangles on a 0 field, with single red (2) cells scattered both inside and outside those rectangles. Comparing input to output, the only new colour is 1. Every patch of 1 is a 3×3 ring centred on a red cell.\n\n**Which red cells get a ring?** Only those lying inside the bounding box of a colour-3 rectangle. This includes reds on the rectangle's own edge, for example (4,2) and (11,2) in pair 1, and the corner red (13,8) in pair 2. Reds outside the rectangles, even ones touching a 3, get nothing. Examples are (1,14) and (4,14) in pair 1, and (19,17) in pair 2.\n\n**How is the ring painted?** All 8 neighbours of a qualifying red become 1, whether they were 3 or 0. A neighbour that is itself a qualifying red stays red. This is why adjacent reds such as (5,10) and (5,11) in pair 3 share one merged ring. A non-qualifying red under a ring is overwritten: pair 1's single 2→1 change is at (12,1), inside the ring of (11,2).\n\n**Construction.**\n1. Find each colour-3 component and take its bounding box.\n2. Mark the red cells inside any of those boxes as active.\n3. Paint each active red's ring, one stage per rectangle.\n\n**Test input.** There are two 3-rectangles: rows 0–9 × cols 13–24 and rows 12–15 × cols 13–21, separated by zero rows. The large top-left rectangle is the third, at rows 2–10 × cols 3–10. The red cells inside those boxes get rings. Stray reds like (0,5), (3,26) and (15,3) stay untouched unless a ring covers them.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    out = g.copy()\n    H, W = g.shape\n    # rectangles of colour 3\n    rects = [bbox(c) for c in components(g == 3)]\n    active = np.zeros_like(g, dtype=bool)\n    per_rect = []\n    for (y0, x0, y1, x1) in rects:\n        reds = [(y, x) for y in range(y0, y1 + 1) for x in range(x0, x1 + 1) if g[y, x] == 2]\n        for p in reds:\n            active[p] = True\n        per_rect.append(reds)\n    for reds in per_rect:\n        if not reds:\n            continue\n        for (y, x) in reds:\n            for dy in (-1, 0, 1):\n                for dx in (-1, 0, 1):\n                    yy, xx = y + dy, x + dx\n                    if 0 <= yy < H and 0 <= xx < W and not active[yy, xx]:\n                        out[yy, xx] = 1\n        emit(out, \"Drew 3x3 rings of 1 around every red cell lying inside one colour-3 rectangle.\")\n    emit(out, \"Final: all red cells inside rectangles are framed by rings of 1.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"crop":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**Which red cells get a ring?** Only those lying inside the bounding box of a colour-3 rectangle."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Find each colour-3 component and take its bounding box."}],"layers":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The red cells inside those boxes get rings."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Drew 3x3 rings of 1 around every red cell lying inside one colour-3 rectangle."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Final: all red cells inside rectangles are framed by rings of 1."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"0ada1ae710c4050ca0fbf9bd38f8be3f8887b24573e8b5b72346f3bae83534a5","task_sha256":"07b08464059493657467c3b3596d0bbdcecff2d7f65cb2d0029cd92fd50e4b86","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
